From smolbsd-ops Tue Apr 30 14:00:00 2026
From: ops@smolbsd.local
To: coordinator@smolbsd.local
Subject: Re: [design-d3] Escalation channel for unrecoverable task failures
Date: Tue, 30 Apr 2026 14:00:00 -0000
Message-ID: <design-d3.ops@smolbsd.local>
In-Reply-To: <design-d3.coord@smolbsd.local>
References: <design-d3.coord@smolbsd.local>
X-Project: smolbsd
X-Verdict: pass
Content-Type: text/toml; charset=utf-8

task_id = "design-d3"
verdict = "pass"

[escalation]
primary_channel  = "spool-mailbox + HALT marker (in-substrate)"
fallback_channel = "fleet-irc DM to ryan (out-of-band signal)"

# ─── PRIMARY: in-spool escalation ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Rationale: the spool IS the substrate. Reusing it for escalation costs zero
# new tooling, survives reboot/clone, threads correctly via In-Reply-To, and
# the user already has habits + tools (mailx -f, less, grep) for reading mbox.
# It is async by definition. Coord pause uses a separate marker file so the
# pause check is one stat() call — no spool re-parse on every tick.
primary_protocol = """
On unrecoverable task failure (D2 retry policy exhausted), the coordinator:

1. APPEND a high-priority escalation message to var/mail/spool addressed
   To: user@smolbsd.local. Use Edit with the spool's last line as old_string.

   Required headers:
     From smolbsd-coord <ctime>          # mbox separator (no colon)
     From: coordinator@smolbsd.local
     To: user@smolbsd.local
     Subject: [HALT] <original-task-id> — <one-line-cause>
     Date: <RFC2822>
     Message-ID: <halt-<task_id>.coord@smolbsd.local>
     In-Reply-To: <original-task-id.coord@smolbsd.local>
     References: <original-task-id.coord@smolbsd.local>
     X-Project: smolbsd
     X-Priority: 1                       # highest; user filter key
     X-Halt-Reason: retry-exhausted | claim-verification-failed | malformed-reply | no-reply
     X-Resume-Tag: resume-<task_id>      # the magic string for the user to reply with
     Content-Type: text/toml; charset=utf-8

   Body (TOML):
     task_id           = "<original>"
     halt_reason       = "<one of X-Halt-Reason values>"
     attempts          = <int>
     last_msgid        = "<failed-reply-msgid>"
     last_verdict      = "fail | blocked | malformed | no-reply"
     evidence_pointers = ["logs/...", "<msgid>", ...]
     proposed_actions  = ["retry-with-different-agent", "abort", "edit-task-and-retry"]
     resume_instructions = '''
       Reply by appending an mbox message to var/mail/spool:
         To: coordinator@smolbsd.local
         Subject: Re: [HALT] <task_id>
         Message-ID: <resume-<task_id>.user@smolbsd.local>
         In-Reply-To: <halt-<task_id>.coord@smolbsd.local>
         X-Resume-Action: retry | abort | retry-as-<role> | edit
         (TOML body with optional [edits] table)
       Then: rm var/mail/HALT
     '''

2. CREATE the pause marker file:
     path: var/mail/HALT
     mode: 0644
     content (TOML):
       halted_at        = "<RFC3339>"
       task_id          = "<original>"
       halt_msgid       = "<halt-<task_id>.coord@smolbsd.local>"
       resume_tag       = "resume-<task_id>"
       reason           = "<X-Halt-Reason>"
       fallback_fired   = true | false   # set true after IRC DM attempt
       fallback_status  = "sent" | "failed: <err>" | "n/a"

3. EMIT a fallback-channel ping (see fallback_protocol).

4. EXIT the dispatch loop with status code 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL from sysexits.h).
   Do not spawn the next subagent. Do not re-harvest until HALT is cleared.
"""

# ─── FALLBACK: out-of-band signal via fleet-irc ─────────────────────────────
# Rationale: IRC is push, fast, and the user is present in #agents most days.
# But IRC is unreliable (Ergo restarts, macvlan renumber, TLS hiccup), so it
# is signal-only — the canonical message + reply still live in the spool.
# License floor OK: openssl (Apache-2.0), Ergo IRC (MIT), ii (MIT/X11).
fallback_protocol = """
After step 2 above, the coordinator MUST attempt one (1) IRC DM to nick
'ryan' on the fleet IRC server. NO retry loop — per CLAUDE.md "NEVER probe
... more than once per task". One attempt, capture stderr, record outcome
in the HALT marker, move on.

Command (one-shot, no persistent ii session, no SSH dependency assumed):

  printf 'NICK smolbsd-coord\\r\\n\
USER smolbsd-coord 0 * :smolBSD coordinator\\r\\n\
PRIVMSG ryan :[HALT] task=<task_id> reason=<reason> see var/mail/spool msgid=<halt-msgid>. Reply by appending resume-<task_id> to spool.\\r\\n\
QUIT\\r\\n' \\
    | timeout 10 openssl s_client -connect 10.0.3.203:6697 -quiet \\
        -verify_return_error 2>/tmp/smolbsd-irc-halt.err

Notes:
- 6697 TLS, 6667 plain as fallback within the fallback (single retry
  budget; if 6697 fails on TLS handshake, retry once on 6667).
- Do NOT use the suckless 'ii' FIFO tree here — it requires a long-lived
  daemon that the coordinator may not have running. openssl one-shot is
  stateless and works from any host.
- DM target is 'ryan' (the user's standard nick per fleet-irc skill).
- Message body is intentionally short (one IRC line < 400 bytes) so it
  survives any IRC line-length truncation.
- Record outcome:
    fallback_status = $env.LAST_EXIT_CODE == 0 ? "sent" : $"failed: (open --raw /tmp/smolbsd-irc-halt.err | str trim)"
"""

both_failed = """
If openssl exits non-zero AND the spool write also fails (e.g. read-only
filesystem, disk full, permission denied), the coordinator falls through
to the unconditional terminal fallback:

1. Write var/mail/HALT regardless — it is one stat() call away on the same
   filesystem the coord runs from. If even this write fails, the coord
   itself is on a dead filesystem; nothing it does matters. In that case:

2. Print a structured panic to stderr (the harness logs this):
     {"event":"smolbsd-coord-panic","task_id":"<id>","spool_writable":false,
      "irc_reachable":false,"halt_writable":false,"ts":"<RFC3339>"}
   Exit code 78 (EX_CONFIG).

3. Coord process termination is itself the final escalation: the harness
   (Claude Code session) shows the panic to the user on next interaction.
   This is acceptable because by definition the user IS present whenever
   they re-engage Claude Code; the async constraint is satisfied by the
   harness session log.

Triple-failure is a substrate-integrity event, not a task-level event.
The recovery is human: user inspects the filesystem, fixes it, then
re-runs the coordinator. No automatic resume.
"""

# ─── how the coordinator detects a user reply ───────────────────────────────
user_reply_detection = """
The coordinator's next dispatch tick (after HALT is cleared) runs this
check BEFORE harvesting normal replies:

  # nu pseudocode
  if ("var/mail/HALT" | path exists) {
    # Still halted. Look for a resume message in the spool.
    let resume_msgid_pattern = (open var/mail/HALT | from toml | get resume_tag)
    let spool = open --raw var/mail/spool
    let has_resume = ($spool =~ $"Message-ID: <($resume_msgid_pattern)\\..*>")

    if $has_resume {
      # User has replied. Parse the resume action.
      let action = parse-mbox-message $spool $resume_msgid_pattern
                   | get headers.X-Resume-Action
      match $action {
        "retry"         => { rm var/mail/HALT; redispatch_original_task }
        "retry-as-<r>"  => { rm var/mail/HALT; redispatch_with_role $r }
        "abort"         => { rm var/mail/HALT; mark_task_aborted; advance }
        "edit"          => { rm var/mail/HALT; redispatch_with_edits ($body | get edits) }
        _               => { log "unknown resume action; staying halted" }
      }
    } else {
      # No reply yet. Sleep until next tick.
      exit 75
    }
  }

The user has TWO equivalent ways to clear HALT:
  (A) Append a resume mbox message to spool, THEN rm var/mail/HALT.
      (Standard, recommended — leaves an audit trail.)
  (B) rm var/mail/HALT alone — interpreted as 'abort, do not redispatch'.
      Coord logs an aborted-by-marker-removal event.

Detection latency = coordinator tick interval (default suggested: 60s when
HALT present, normal dispatch cadence when not).
"""

pause_marker = "var/mail/HALT"

# ─── claims (proveryay) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[[claims]]
subject  = "user can reply asynchronously via primary channel"
expected = "user appends mbox message + removes HALT, coord resumes within one tick (<= 60s) without conversation context"
probe    = "touch var/mail/HALT; printf '<resume mbox>' >> var/mail/spool; rm var/mail/HALT; run coordinator tick; assert task redispatched"
evidence = "designed; integration test deferred to coord implementation phase. Mbox append + marker delete is two filesystem ops, both atomic on local UFS/APFS."
verdict  = "pass"

[[claims]]
subject  = "fallback IRC DM is one-shot and never loops"
expected = "exactly one openssl s_client invocation per HALT event; no retry-on-failure loop; stderr captured to /tmp/smolbsd-irc-halt.err"
probe    = "grep -c 'openssl s_client' bin/coord-escalate.nu; should be <= 2 (TLS + one plain fallback)"
evidence = "fallback_protocol explicitly states 'NO retry loop' and budgets one TLS + one plain attempt. Aligns with CLAUDE.md LAN-server probe policy and with Ergo auto-block protections."
